<p>I am building <a href="https://github.com/EmericBraud/alcyon">Alcyon</a>, a chess engine written in C++23. The main purpose of this blog is to record what I learn along the way.</p>

<p>A chess engine is a great place to learn, because it forgives nothing:</p>

<ul>
<li>algorithmic complexity becomes tangible: every optimization shows up as search depth gained;</li>
<li>the cost of a memory access stops being a detail the day a lookup table, meant to avoid a computation, turns out more expensive than that computation because it does not fit in the cache;</li>
<li>parallelism shows its real limits when synchronization costs more than it brings;</li>
<li>nothing can be claimed without measurement: an improvement can only be proven statistically, over thousands of games.</li>
</ul>

<p>These problems go well beyond chess: they arise in any program where performance and correctness matter.</p>

<p>The articles therefore start from chess, but aim to explore the underlying programming concepts. The first section explains how a chess engine works in general, and sets the context of the project. The following sections explore more general concepts, and can be read independently.</p>

